KZ3 patch 2140 (RSX pass 132): LD_NV3089_ROWS=2, row copies for NV3089 blits whose source and destination ranges intersect, default on
(byte-identical to the per-pixel loop; K4: copy time about 5x lower, no frame-rate change shown). LD_NV3089_ROWS=0 restores the per-pixel loop.

The NV3089 guest copy is the whole cost of the blit method in Killzone 3's frozen_shores level: about 90 blits and 78 MB a
second (4.4 MB a frame), 44 ms of copy against 0.1 ms for everything else in the method ([perf-nv3089]), i.e. the
per-pixel vm_read32 + vm_write32 loop at 1.8 GB/s. LD_NV3089_ROWS=1 (patch 2080) copies rows with memcpy but declines
every blit whose source and destination byte ranges merely intersect (a texture-pool compaction copies 1024 x 1002
pixels to an address 170 KB below the source), so with rows on the copy time only fell from 43.5 to 40 ms a second.

LD_NV3089_ROWS=2 (rsx_nv3089_rows.h nv3089_rows_copy_x with exact_overlap) keeps the 12.4 origin, clip, 4 GB wrap and
store-hook declines of 2080 and replaces the range test by the loop's real hazard. The pixel loop runs rows and pixels in
ascending order, so a row is byte-identical to one memmove unless a pixel reads bytes an earlier pixel of the same row
wrote: delta = destination row start - source row start, 0 < delta < run length, run longer than one pixel. Only such
blits go to the per-pixel loop; rows still run in ascending order. Default 0 (unset = per-pixel loop, 1 = patch 2080).
tools/smoke/test_nv3089_rows.sh compares both rules with the per-pixel loop over 20000 random blits (bpp 1/2/4, clips,
pitches, overlap, wrap): byte-identical, the exact rule never declines what the range rule copied, 2061 more copied.
Defaults: see the RE notes "Pass 132".

Rebased on ps3recomp v0.19.0 (SPU pass 154): three-way merge into the v0.19.0 chain, hunks kept as before, context follows v0.19 (chain glue, owner lane not re-reviewed).

